// @run
// @log 1
// @log 2
// @log 1
// @log 2
// THE PRICE THE MERGE HAD TO PAY UP FRONT, AND THE ONLY CELL THAT EVER WENT SILENT.
// `type AB = {b: i32} & {a: i32}` merges to a row spelled `{b, a}`; `W.xs` is spelled
// `{a, b}`. Two struct rows collapse to ONE wasm heap type only when their field SEQUENCE
// matches, and `structFieldCodesEq` compares field CODES positionally and never NAMES — so
// the moment `slotCanonId` stopped declining struct-alias-union rows (the D441/D442/D443
// fix), this pair MERGED with `a` and `b` in opposite slots and printed `2 1` instead of
// `1 2`: a check-clean invalid wasm turned into a check-clean WRONG ANSWER.
//
// `collectS`'s `TypeDecl` arm has canonicalised field order against an existing same-
// fieldset row since `xfail-miscompile-permuted-struct-fields.vl`; `internShapeAs` — the
// second mint site, one layer down — did not, and could not be caught doing it because its
// rows had never been eligible to merge. The two sites now share `rowWithFieldNameSet`.
//
// Measured over the 151-cell D402 neighbourhood grid, this is the ONLY cell that any rung of
// that landing moved to a silent answer, and it is the only reason the reorder rung exists:
// on its own the reorder closes no inventory row and moves no graded population at all.
type AB = {b: i32} & {a: i32}
type W = { xs: {a: i32, b: i32}[] }
const q: AB[] = [{ a: 1, b: 2 }]
const w: W = { xs: q }
print(w.xs[0].a)
print(w.xs[0].b)
print(q[0].a)
print(q[0].b)
